berkeley-master.logic.r1: argue/equivocates: A1: "unconceived" is taken as objects actually being unthought of while one is thinking of them (the act of conceiving) in A1 C1 and as conceiving objects as existing with no one perceiving them (what is pictured) where conceive_unconceived is used else, a reader says: the argument holds only if they are one; taken apart, C (S4) does not follow (berkeley-master.logic.r1.sense1)
berkeley-master.logic.r1: argue/countermodel: C (S4): conceive_mind_indep = T, conceive_unconceived = T, conceive_unconceived_2 = F
